sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, DECISIONS R1-R4, 5 (swamp role SSH identity), round 2 bugs (server admin password by its own vault key, cleanup on pass or fail). docs/reference/database-servers.md#remove-a-database.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6, 16, 19, 22 (no copied scripts or cleanup, no ownership read, blocker check and drops in one SQL batch on stdin).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: database:destroy
  %% kind: database.destroy
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as Node 12<br/>beast-mysql server
  CLI->>GW: database:destroy shop
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit database:destroy shop --force`
  %%> - syntax: `<slug> [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the connection slug, as `database:list` shows it
  %%> Confirmation:
  %%> - without `--force`, the CLI asks first; the default is No
  %%> - with `--json` or without a terminal, `--force` is required (`database.confirmation_required`)
  Note over CLI: Confirm destruction<br/>default No
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - ask on a terminal before sending the request; decline cancels
  Note over GW: Check, plan the removal<br/>task #5124 · lock beast-mysql
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12
  %%> - no Instance attaches `shop` (`database.connection_attached`); detach with `instance:database:remove` first
  %%> - AI router setup and Analytics do not use it
  %%> - no database creation or other removal runs on `beast-mysql`; the Gateway holds one lock per Database server
  %%> Plans:
  %%> - the databases: `shop`, its test database and the recorded test pool on MySQL or PostgreSQL
  %%> - the users Orbit created for `shop` that no other connection on the server uses
  %%> - system databases such as `mysql` or `postgres` are refused before any run
  %%> Decides:
  %%> - whether only the record goes, or the database must leave the server
  alt connection without a server
    Note over GW: Delete only the record<br/>R1: no run, database stays
    %%> When:
    %%> - a registered external database or SQLite file
    %%> Result:
    %%> - connection `shop` is deleted
    %%> - the database itself stays where it is; Orbit never created it
    %%> - no Swamp run starts (R1)
    GW-->>CLI: Connection destroyed<br/>database kept
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the final result at once; there is nothing to follow
  else database on a server
    GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-database-destroy
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
    %%> Inputs:
    %%> - the planned databases (with the recorded test database and pool) and unshared users
    %%> - four vault key names, one per batch (`database-destroy-5124-fence`, `-drop`, `-gone`, `-restore`); each value is the admin password line and its statements, so the password never enters the intent
    %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
    GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5124
    %%> What you get:
    %%> - the task number, at once
    %%> Next:
    %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
    opt PostgreSQL server
      SW->>N: Fence new clients<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin
      %%> How:
      %%> - Swamp reaches Node 12 over SSH from 209 with the official `@swamp/ssh` behavior; nothing is copied to the Node
      %%> - one `docker exec -i` into the server container, the admin password as the first stdin line, then the statements
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - stops new clients from connecting to every planned database
      %%> - journals each database's prior value in `orbit_admin.database_removal_fences`, in the same transaction
      %%> Not included:
      %%> - existing clients stay connected; Orbit never force-disconnects them
    end
    SW->>N: Drop databases and users<br/>docker exec -i · SQL on stdin
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - one statement batch on stdin: first the blocker query, then each planned database, then each planned user that no other connection on `beast-mysql` uses
    %%> - a missing database or user counts as removed
    %%> Blocks:
    %%> - PostgreSQL: sessions, replication slots, prepared transactions, subscriptions, outside dependencies of a removed role
    %%> - MySQL: connected clients, metadata locks, outside definers, cross-schema foreign keys
    %%> - any blocker ends the batch with `database.in_use` before the first drop
    %%> Per driver:
    %%> - admin login: the admin password as the first stdin line, from the vault, read inside the container
    %%> - Valkey: no database; `ACL DELUSER`, then `ACL SAVE`; the user's keys stay
    %%> - Valkey default login: the removal plan excludes `default`; its credentials and keys stay
    %%> Not included:
    %%> - no ownership read first: the plan comes from the Gateway's records
    SW->>N: Check they are gone<br/>docker exec -i · SELECT
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - checks that no planned database or user is left on the server; the check fails the run while one remains
    %%> Why:
    %%> - the removal only counts when the server shows it; this is the one end check, and a parent run such as `instance:destroy` sees a refusal too
    opt a step failed
      SW->>N: Restore the fence<br/>prior ALLOW_CONNECTIONS
      %%> When:
      %%> - only after a failed step on a fenced PostgreSQL server: a step with `dependsOn` and the condition `failed`
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - gives each surviving database its exact prior value from the journal
      %%> If it fails:
      %%> - the journal stays; the next `database:destroy` restores or finishes
    end
    alt every step passes
      N-->>SW: set absent
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the check passed for every database and user
      SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
      %%> How the Gateway learns it:
      %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
      Note over GW: Delete the connection<br/>users too · task succeeded
      %%> Result:
      %%> - connection `shop` and its recorded users are deleted
      %%> - task #5124 succeeds
    else blocked or a drop fails
      N--xSW: in use or exit ≠ 0
      %%> When:
      %%> - a blocker, an ownership refusal, a failed drop, or a lost SSH connection
      SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
      %%> The Gateway reads:
      %%> - which step failed and its error code, for example `database.in_use`
      Note over GW: Keep the connection<br/>task failed · rerun after a fix
      %%> Result:
      %%> - connection `shop` stays with its users and attachments; the task records the failure
      %%> - task #5124 fails
      %%> On the Node:
      %%> - after a blocker, nothing was dropped
      %%> - after a failed drop, the dropped objects stay gone; there is no rollback
      %%> Retry:
      %%> - stop the clients or remove the blockers, then run the same command again
      %%> - a rerun repeats the checks and counts missing objects as removed
    end
    loop every 2 s until terminal
      CLI->>GW: operation:show #5124
      %%> What it does:
      %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
      GW-->>CLI: status + result
      %%> Returns:
      %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
    end
  end
